## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/gb.svg" width="32" alt="United Kingdom" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />United Kingdom

The United Kingdom continues implementing its bilateral web strategy through concrete China engagement delivering visa-free travel and £10.9bn investment commitments while managing direct Trump criticism of UK-China business relationships. Domestic regime fragmentation accelerates simultaneously across governing party (leadership protection prioritized over electoral competitiveness through Burnham blocking), opposition (Conservative explicit abandonment of centrism with continued Reform UK defections), and monarchy (King Charles's unprecedented political documentary breaking constitutional neutrality). The convergence of successful external diversification with internal institutional breakdown creates the paradox of strategic hedging success amid domestic governance crisis.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** Prime Minister Starmer completed the first UK leader visit to China in eight years, announcing visa-free travel for British citizens, trade agreements, and securing AstraZeneca's £10.9 billion China investment commitment through 2030. Trump called UK-China business deals 'very dangerous,' marking direct US criticism of the engagement strategy.
- **Domestic:** Labour's National Executive Committee voted to block Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham from standing in the Gorton and Denton by-election, with PM Starmer personally voting against his potential rival. The decision triggered accusations of internal 'civil war' and concerns about electoral prospects.
- **Domestic:** King Charles III's Amazon Prime documentary 'Finding Harmony: A King's Vision' premiered at Windsor Castle, marking an unprecedented project for a British monarch and breaking tradition of avoiding political topics. The environmental advocacy film represents direct political engagement by the constitutional head of state.
- **Domestic:** Following Suella Braverman's defection to Reform UK, Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch declared centrist ideas 'no longer wanted' in the party and told dissenters to 'get out of the way.' The rightward shift explicitly rejects the historically strong centrist tradition in conservatism.
- **Security:** UK Ministry of Defence directly awarded a three-year £240.6 million contract to Palantir UK for continued licensing and support of data analytics capabilities. The contract covers strategic, tactical and operational decision-making across defense classifications and NATO interoperability.
- **Unexpected:** King Charles releases first-ever streaming platform documentary with explicit political content.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Starmer visits China seeking economic reset amid Trump tensions** — Prime Minister Keir Starmer made the first visit to China by a British leader in eight years, meeting Xi Jinping and announcing visa-free travel for Brits. The trip faced criticism from Trump who called UK-China business deals 'very dangerous', while Starmer defended pursuing closer economic ties. *([abcnews.go.com](https://reuters.com/world/china/uks-starmer-heads-china-repair-ties-he-navigates-tensions-with-us-2026-01-27))*
- **Conservative MPs defect to Reform UK amid party direction disputes** — Former Home Secretary Suella Braverman defected to Reform UK, following Robert Jenrick's earlier switch. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch responded by saying centrist ideas are 'no longer wanted' in the party and that dissenters should 'get out of the way', marking a rightward shift in Conservative positioning. *([theguardian.com](https://www.reuters.com/world/uk/british-lawmaker-braverman-defects-farages-reform-uk-2026-01-26/))*
- **Rachel Reeves faces questions over absence from China trip and policy pressures** — Chancellor Rachel Reeves was notably absent from Starmer's high-profile China visit despite previously advocating for stronger economic ties. Speculation arose about tensions with the PM, while she separately faced criticism over student loan threshold freezes and business rate changes affecting pubs. *([dailymail.co.uk](https://dailymail.co.uk/news/article-15505325/Mystery-Rache-Reeves-Chancellor-absence-China-trip-tensions-Starmer.html))*
- **David Lammy stands in for Starmer at PMQs, faces Conservative attacks** — Deputy Prime Minister David Lammy stepped in for Prime Minister's Questions while Starmer was in China, facing criticism from Conservative shadow business secretary Andrew Griffith about pub closures and business rates. Lammy was dubbed 'left-behind Lammy' by opponents. *([bbc.co.uk](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/c4gw427eenpt))*
- **Bank of England expected to hold interest rates amid inflation concerns** — Economists predict the Bank of England will keep interest rates at 3.75% at its February meeting as inflation rose to 3.4% in December. The central bank also faces cost-cutting pressures, leading to staff reorganization and potential job reductions. *([bloomberg.com](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-30/even-the-bank-of-england-is-struggling-to-make-ends-meet))*
- **Starmer faces migrant protests and calls Andrew to testify on Epstein links** — Thousands protested in Crowborough chanting 'Keir Starmer's a traitor' over migrants being housed at a former army camp. Separately, Starmer called for Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor to testify before US Congress about his Jeffrey Epstein connections following release of new Epstein files. *([dailymail.co.uk](https://dailymail.co.uk/news/article-15496219/Thousands-chant-Starmers-traitor-march-Crowborough-protesting-migrants.html))*
- **King Charles makes public appearances amid ongoing cancer treatment** — King Charles attended the Sandringham parkrun for the second consecutive year, greeting joggers as they completed their first lap. The event was organized in partnership with Move Against Cancer charity. The King has been receiving cancer treatment since early 2024. *([news.sky.com](https://news.sky.com/story/the-king-greets-joggers-in-surprise-appearance-at-cancer-charity-parkrun-13501390))*
- **King Charles faces calls to formally apologise for crown's role in slavery** — MPs and campaigners called on King Charles to make a formal apology for transatlantic slavery after new research detailed how the British crown and Royal Navy extended and protected the trade in enslaved people for centuries. The calls follow publication of 'The Crown's Silence' book. *([theguardian.com](https://theguardian.com/news/2026/jan/30/calls-for-king-charles-to-formally-apologise-for-slavery-after-research-shows-crowns-role))*
- **Reform UK campaigns in Gorton by-election with attacks on Starmer's Brexit policy** — Reform UK candidate Matt Goodwin declared the Gorton and Denton by-election a 'referendum on Starmer', claiming Brexit voters have been betrayed. The campaign focuses on immigration and higher bills, with Goodwin arguing Labour is edging Britain back under EU control through customs deals and youth mobility schemes. *([bloomberg.com](https://bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-28/starmer-attack-on-reform-candidate-shows-his-future-on-ballot))*
- **Starmer criticized over historical legal pursuit of British soldiers** — Former Army chief Lord Dannatt called on PM Keir Starmer to explain his involvement in legal cases that saw hundreds of British soldiers investigated for alleged war crimes. Critics questioned Starmer's role and enthusiasm for pursuing these cases during his legal career. *([gbnews.com](https://gbnews.com/politics/keir-starmer-british-soldiers-explain-himself))*
